answer=According to the article, *Sailor Moon* helped reshape and expand magical-hero storytelling by blending the magical girl genre with team-based superhero action. It says the series “became a fusion of the popular magical girl genre and the Super Sentai series,” and that its “dynamic heroines and action-oriented plots” are “widely credited with reinvigorating the magical girl genre.”

The article also notes its broader influence: after *Sailor Moon*, “many similar magical girl series” emerged, and it is credited with popularizing the idea of “a Super Sentai-like team of magical girls.” In short, it contributed by making magical-hero stories more ensemble-based, more action-driven, and centered on “credible, charismatic and independent” female heroes.
